package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story about the radio station in room six. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch46 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_radio_station_in_room_six",
            title = "The Radio Station in Room Six",
            tagline = "A quiet school media club revives an old student broadcast and discovers the value of listening.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "Static at Lunch", listOf(
                    "Room Six had been used for storage since the school moved its media equipment to a newer building. When Kosi joined the journalism club, she found a small radio mixer beneath a stack of cardboard boxes. A label read SCHOOL VOICE—ESTABLISHED 1996. The teacher said the station had once broadcast lunchtime announcements through speakers across the campus.",
                    "The club asked permission to inspect the equipment with the school's technician. Most of it was outdated, but a box of tapes and scripts had been stored in a dry cabinet. Kosi found a handwritten schedule with a blank space every Wednesday. Beside it was a note: 'Leave room for listeners.' She wondered what that meant."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Old Schedule", listOf(
                    "The scripts showed that the station had carried news, music chosen by students, event notices, and short interviews. The Wednesday slot was called Open Mic, but no recordings were listed. Kosi's friend Tunde thought the tapes might have been erased. Their teacher, Mrs Danjuma, advised them not to play unknown media on old equipment without the technician's help.",
                    "The technician checked the tapes and found that some could be digitised safely using approved equipment. The club listened to a sample recording of a school assembly. At the end, a presenter invited students to submit questions anonymously for a weekly discussion. The Open Mic slot had not been empty; it had been reserved for questions from the audience."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "Questions Without Names", listOf(
                    "A folder contained old question cards, with names removed before they were kept. Students had asked about study spaces, sports equipment, school rules, and how to join clubs. The station team had answered some questions and passed others to staff. Kosi liked the idea that a broadcast could make it easier for quieter students to be heard.",
                    "The club proposed restarting the programme with clear rules: no personal accusations, no sharing private details, and no reading a question until a staff adviser had reviewed it. The head teacher approved a four-week trial. The station would use the school's internal audio system, not an unrestricted public stream. The team would also provide a written question box for students who preferred not to speak."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "A Voice from the Archive", listOf(
                    "One digitised recording featured a presenter named Nneka, who explained that questions were selected because they could help many students. The recording mentioned a student council project to create a quiet study corner. Kosi found a public school report confirming that the corner had opened the following year, but it did not explain how the idea began.",
                    "The alumni office helped contact Nneka, now a librarian. She remembered the programme and agreed to speak with the club. She said the station had been most useful when presenters listened carefully and followed up with staff. The students invited her to advise their trial, and she offered to share a checklist for responsible school journalism."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The First Broadcast", listOf(
                    "The club gathered questions for its first show. Several students asked about library hours, the location of lost property, and how to propose a new club. Kosi prepared short answers using information confirmed by staff. One question raised a concern about a crowded corridor, so Mrs Danjuma arranged for the school safety team to review it rather than letting students speculate on air.",
                    "The first broadcast lasted eight minutes. Tunde read the announcements, Kosi explained how to submit questions, and another student described the process for starting a club. The team made a mistake when they gave an outdated library closing time. They corrected it on the noticeboard immediately and added a rule that every answer must have a named source and date."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Wednesday Gap", listOf(
                    "As the trial continued, the question box filled quickly. The students noticed that many questions were similar, especially about finding help with schoolwork. They asked the librarian whether the station could feature a weekly study tip. She agreed, but said advice should be practical and should not replace asking a teacher when someone needed individual support.",
                    "Nneka visited and explained the old Wednesday gap. It had been left open so students could hear their own concerns reflected in the programme, not just the presenters' favourite topics. She encouraged the club to keep some time for questions that did not fit a prepared script. Kosi added a short listener segment, with every contribution checked for privacy and respectful language."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "A Rumour on the Air", listOf(
                    "A card arrived claiming that a teacher had cancelled an important school trip without telling anyone. The club could not verify the claim, and the teacher's name was written on the card. Tunde suggested reading it as a question, but Kosi worried it could spread an unfair rumour. Mrs Danjuma agreed: they would not broadcast it.",
                    "Instead, the club asked the trips coordinator for a general update that could be shared with everyone. The coordinator explained that the date was being reviewed because transport confirmation was pending. The station announced only that verified information would be posted when available. The student who submitted the card later thanked the team for answering the concern without identifying anyone."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "A Better Sound", listOf(
                    "The technician helped the club improve the room's sound setup and showed them how to check levels safely. The students created a simple recording checklist and saved each programme with its date, presenters, and sources. They also prepared a transcript for students who could not hear the internal broadcast clearly.",
                    "The head teacher reviewed the trial and praised the corrections log. She asked the club to keep the programme short and ensure that announcements were available in writing. Kosi suggested rotating presenters so the station would not depend on one person. The club agreed to train new members and maintain a shared schedule."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Listening Assembly", listOf(
                    "At the end of the trial, the school held an assembly about student communication. Nneka spoke about the original station, and Kosi played a short approved excerpt from the archive. The school announced that the radio club would continue for the year, with a staff adviser and clear privacy rules. Students could submit questions through the box or a supervised school form.",
                    "The principal also approved a monthly response sheet showing which suggestions had been considered, which had been acted on, and which could not be adopted. The club understood that listening did not mean promising every request would happen. It meant giving people a fair way to be heard and explaining decisions honestly."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "Room Six On Air", listOf(
                    "Room Six was no longer storage. The old mixer remained on a shelf as part of the school's history display, while the current broadcasts used reliable equipment checked by the technician. Kosi trained two younger students to manage the question box and source list. Tunde created a guide so future members could continue the programme without guessing at the rules.",
                    "On the final Wednesday of term, the station left a full minute for listener questions. The first was simple: 'Can we have more time to read?' The librarian replied that a quiet reading period would be tested next month. Kosi looked at the old schedule and understood its blank space. It had never been empty. It had been waiting for someone to listen. Room Six had found its voice by making space for everyone else's."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
